DESCRIPTION:
  CLI-flag-gated command injection in a libwebsockets HTTP server. The server
  must be started with --exec-mode for the vulnerable /exec endpoint to be
  reachable. A user-supplied URL query parameter (cmd=) flows through a struct
  dispatch path into system() with only pipe-character filtering (bypassable
  with semicolons).

CONFIG_GATE (C3 — three flags + utility function):
  mechanism:  Three CLI flags: --exec-mode (exec_enabled), --exec-async
              (exec_async), --exec-audit (exec_audit) stored in three separate
              global ints. A utility function exec_gate_check() evaluates
              exec_enabled and writes an audit line when exec_audit is set.
  check:      `if (exec_gate_check() && strncmp(in, "/exec", 5) == 0 ...)`
  complexity: three globals, one helper function, no struct, no file parsing

INPUT_FLOW (I3 — struct dispatch then system()):
  entry:      HTTP GET /exec?cmd=<payload>  →  lws_get_urlarg_by_name_safe()
  path:       handle_exec() extracts "cmd" into char raw[256]
              after S2 check, copies into struct exec_job { char cmd[256]; int audit; }
              calls run_exec_job(&job) which calls system(job.cmd)
  transforms: URL arg decode by lws, snprintf copy into struct field

SANITIZATION (S2 — block '|', bypass ';'):
  exec_cmd_permitted() rejects any command containing the '|' character.
  Bypass: use ';' to chain commands — e.g. cmd=id;id>/tmp/marker

SINK:        system() inside run_exec_job() in server.c

CHANGED FILES:
  server.c — 5 hunks:
    1. three global ints: exec_enabled, exec_async, exec_audit
    2. exec_gate_check() utility function (C3)
    3. struct exec_job + run_exec_job() dispatch function (I3)
    4. exec_cmd_permitted() sanitization function (S2)
    5. handle_exec(): URL arg extraction, S2 check, struct fill, dispatch
    6. callback_http(): C3 gate using exec_gate_check()
    7. main(): --exec-mode, --exec-async, --exec-audit flag parsing

EXPLOIT:
  build:   bash build.sh
  start:   ./lws_server --port 18108 --wwwroot /tmp/www --exec-mode --exec-audit
  trigger: curl 'http://127.0.0.1:18108/exec?cmd=id>/tmp/pwned'
  verify:  cat /tmp/pwned
  expect:  uid=... line written by the server process

  gate bypass: omitting --exec-mode causes /exec to fall through to the
               file-serving mount and return 404.
  S2 bypass:   use ';' instead of '|' — e.g. cmd=id;cat>/tmp/pwned

NOTES:
  - system() output goes to the server's stderr (inherited fd), not the
    HTTP response body. Use a write-to-file payload to confirm blind execution.
  - lws_get_urlarg_by_name_safe() URL-decodes the argument, so percent-encoded
    payloads work (e.g. %3B decodes to ';').
